it's time for us to go and speak
with Thadeus about Hand Talk.

00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:50.800
MARK MANDEL: Let's do it.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.260
MELANIE WARRICK: This week we
have with us Thadeu Luz, who

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.330
is from Hand Talk,
who's here to talk to us

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.810
about what Hand Talk does
and how it's using GCP.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.344
Thadeu, thanks for joining us.

00:04:01.344 --> 00:04:02.260
THADEU LUZ: Thank you.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:02.980
MELANIE WARRICK:
Thadeu, can you tell us

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.820
a little bit about the company?

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.650
What does Hand Talk do?

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.550
THADEU LUZ: So I'm a founder
and CPO at Hand Talk,

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:13.430
which is a startup based here
in the city of Maceió in Brazil,

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.774
which is on the coast and very
warm weather and nice beaches.

00:04:16.774 --> 00:04:17.690
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.481
THADEU LUZ: And I'm
also a Google developer

00:04:19.481 --> 00:04:21.860
expert in Firebase.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.720
Hand Talk is a company that
offers automatic sign language

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.370
interpretation solutions for
users and companies as well.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.450
And we do that through a
friendly virtual 3D character

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.386
named Hugo.

00:04:36.386 --> 00:04:39.010
MELANIE WARRICK: So how did you
guys come up with this company?

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:44.170
THADEU LUZ: OK, so Hand Talk
started when, in 2011, I

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.610
came back from Canada.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.570
I was living in Toronto
for two years with my wife

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.910
and working on the VFX industry.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.460
That's actually my background,
in computer graphics

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.590
and digital compositing.

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:00.100
And when I came back, a couple
of friends invited me to help

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.830
with this project they
were working on, which was

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.370
a prototype for a demo day
they were going to have here

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.410
in Maceió.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:09.610
And the idea was to
come up with an app that

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.750
would translate to sign language
using a virtual character.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.330
And they needed someone
that had experience

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:20.430
with character design and 3D
animation to finish him off,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:21.500
so that's how I started.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.000
MELANIE WARRICK:
How did the company

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.520
decide to work on
from translating

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.960
spoken and written Portuguese
into Brazilian Sign Language?

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.800
THADEU LUZ: This idea was
a school project, actually,

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:37.700
from my co-founder and
my partner, Ronaldo.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.290
He's a CEO now.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.330
He had this idea for
a very long time.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.010
He came up with this product
when he was in university,

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:49.460
and he needed to do that
to come up with a marketing

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.130
campaign for the
product, so he thought

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:56.000
of doing something at
the time which was using

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.790
desktop computers or notebooks.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:03.020
And this was a long time ago,
in 2004, even before the iPhone.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.270
So after that, we
thought that this

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.420
could work for mobile devices.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:11.060
And we did this demo
for this contest here,

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:15.080
this local contest in Maceió,
and we won the first prize.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.730
Everybody loved Hugo.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:20.120
And we used the money
as seed investment

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.600
to open up the company.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.440
And that's what we've been
working on ever since.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.340
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.160
Can you tell us more
about the problem too?

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.104
Is the translation from,
say, Portuguese or even just

00:06:31.104 --> 00:06:33.020
any sort of written
language to sign language,

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.144
is that kind of one-to-one?

00:06:34.144 --> 00:06:36.080
Or how complicated is that?

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.940
THADEU LUZ: OK, so
that's a good question,

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.060
because it's not
at all one-to-one.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:46.100
Sign languages are languages by
themselves, proper languages.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.760
They're not signed English
or signed Portuguese.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.550
They have a structure and
a grammar and morphology

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.050
that's specific to them.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.360
So when we first started, we
thought that the hardest part

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.500
would be to animate a
language, because there

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.290
are quite a few signs.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.120
In Brazilian Sign
Language, I think

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:11.330
it's estimated it's over 40,000.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:12.290
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:13.280
THADEU LUZ: And we
thought it would

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.110
be really hard to
animate a language,

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:19.430
but the actual hard part is
to restructure the grammar

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:21.851
and make it work as
a different language.

00:07:21.851 --> 00:07:24.350
MARK MANDEL: So it sounds like
this is very similar to, say,

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.720
a translation between French
and English or English

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.690
and Portuguese as much as
it is like a spoken language

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:30.860
to a sign language.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:31.760
THADEU LUZ: Exactly.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.300
One other important
thing to note

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.690
is that there are over
200 different sign

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.130
languages in the world today.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:41.120
Brazilian Sign Language, or
Libras, what I'll call it

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:44.510
is the Brazilian
official sign language.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:49.470
But in the US, you guys use
ASL, and it's important, also,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.500
to say that there
is no relationship

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.520
between the oral language of a
country and the sign language

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:56.420
that they use.

00:07:56.420 --> 00:08:00.890
ASL is used in Canada,
and Mexico as well,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:04.790
in some parts of Central
America, Asia, in Africa--

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:08.716
ASL is actually the most popular
sign language in the world.

00:08:08.716 --> 00:08:09.590
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:12.950
In terms of the app itself,
how can you access it?

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:13.922
How is it used?

00:08:13.922 --> 00:08:15.380
THADEU LUZ: The
app is free to use.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:19.040
You can find it on Google
Play and Apple's App Store.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.260
And you can download
it, and if you're

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.930
interested in learning
sign language for now,

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:26.510
you can just type or
say whatever you want,

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.890
and Hugo will sign
it back to you.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.440
We also have a
couple of sessions

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:33.799
where you can get started
with sign language,

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:37.970
learning groups of signs
like fruits and colors

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:40.024
if you're new to
Brazilian Sign Language.

00:08:40.024 --> 00:08:40.940
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:43.309
Well, in terms of
the app, why Hugo?

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:46.850
Why was the avatar designed the
way it was and Hugo the name?

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.280
THADEU LUZ: When I
was designing him,

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:52.530
I wasn't really sure
how he would look like.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:56.960
But I was pretty clear of what
I wanted him to represent.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.980
So we were making
this character that

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.230
would be not the mascot
but the product itself.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.990
I wanted him to have the
mission of the company.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:11.120
And I wanted him to look
friendly and also credible,

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.510
because that's what we were
aiming for as a company.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.650
And also, I wanted him to be
specific for sign language

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:19.980
communication.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.490
So I gave him a few
characteristics like long

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:27.020
limbs, and large hands, and
also a large face for facial

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.490
expressions , because
this is really,

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:32.300
really important for
communication in sign language.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.210
And as for the name, it
was my wife that named him.

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.990
I had picked this placeholder
name just at random

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:43.190
so that I could name the files
in the geometry and things

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:43.910
like that.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.740
And she came up
one day and said,

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:47.170
he doesn't look like that.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:48.055
He looks like a Hugo.

00:09:48.055 --> 00:09:48.930
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS]

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:49.035
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:09:49.035 --> 00:09:50.660
THADEU LUZ: And
when she said it,

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:53.090
it was pretty obvious
that she was right.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:56.162
Everybody agreed, and
the name really stuck.

00:09:56.162 --> 00:09:57.620
MELANIE WARRICK:
I love the details

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:58.570
that you have to think about.

00:09:58.570 --> 00:09:59.986
I mean, that's
impressive to think

00:09:59.986 --> 00:10:02.720
about it in terms of the design
of the character itself--

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.677
the long limbs, the large
hands, the facial expressions.

00:10:05.677 --> 00:10:07.760
These are things people
wouldn't normally consider

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.040
but you have to consider
when you're doing design.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:11.840
THADEU LUZ: Yeah,
and even the clothes

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:16.100
that he's wearing went
through quite a few iterations

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.440
to get to something
that wasn't very formal

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.280
but at the same time
wasn't too casual.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.840
And he has a tie,
but not a suit.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:25.929
I think we got it right.

00:10:25.929 --> 00:10:27.220
MARK MANDEL: That sounds great.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:30.240
I'd love to get stuck in
more into how the translation

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.242
process is made.

00:10:32.242 --> 00:10:34.200
You kind of hit it like
you just dropped it in,

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.699
and I was like,
wow, that's amazing,

00:10:35.699 --> 00:10:38.190
in that the translation is
not just the hand expressions,

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.870
but also the facial
expressions as well.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.980
So I'd love to hear more
about how that works.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:42.646
THADEU LUZ: Yes.

00:10:42.646 --> 00:10:45.600
Facial expressions are really
important in sign language.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.060
They're basically the
difference between a question--

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.910
I'm not sure about ASL, I'm
talking about Brazilian Sign

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:53.160
Language, which I know for
sure-- that the difference

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.520
between a question
and an affirmation.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.890
So if you do the
same sign, and you

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.110
don't do the facial
expression, you

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.035
can't be really sure if the
person is saying or asking.

00:11:04.035 --> 00:11:05.490
MARK MANDEL: So
it's like a tone.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:08.760
THADEU LUZ: Yes, and also
the facial expressions

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.220
are built on the signs.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.630
Some signs require facial
expressions for them to work.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.570
Some signs are only
facial expressions.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:21.330
It's hard to explain here
because people are not

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.650
going to have the
visual, but there

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:25.950
are some signs that are
just a facial expression.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.450
MELANIE WARRICK:
What's one that you

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.277
would say that is a facial
expression that's a sign?

00:11:30.277 --> 00:11:31.110
THADEU LUZ: "Thief."

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.470
The sign for "thief" in
Brazilian Sign Language

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:34.909
is like this.

00:11:34.909 --> 00:11:36.450
MARK MANDEL: He's
sticking his tongue

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:37.440
into the side of his mouth.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:38.398
THADEU LUZ: Yeah, yeah.

00:11:38.398 --> 00:11:38.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:41.130
If you do this, it's
like a discreet way

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.884
to say, look, there's a thief.

00:11:42.884 --> 00:11:43.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:44.700
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.033
Yeah, tell us more about how
technologically this works.

00:11:47.033 --> 00:11:49.760
I really want to hear about how
these translations are made.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.560
THADEU LUZ: OK, the translation
happens in two steps,

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.690
and they're quite distinct.

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:58.020
One of them is to
translate the oral language

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:00.090
into what we call gloss.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:05.125
Glossing is a process of writing
a language in another language.

00:12:05.125 --> 00:12:07.140
And that's required
for sign language

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.470
because, unfortunately,
there's no official way

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:12.280
to write in sign language.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.706
There are a couple of
attempts to make that,

00:12:14.706 --> 00:12:20.130
to create symbols to represent
the position and hand

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:24.300
shape of signs, but there's
nothing official yet.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.130
And that's one of
the reasons why

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.380
it's hard to find
data on sign language,

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:31.170
because it's really hard
to represent like we

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:34.140
do with our language letters.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:37.560
The first step is to
convert oral language,

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:42.390
like Portuguese, to gloss,
which is written sign language.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.870
And then we can
convert the gloss

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:50.100
into signs or fingerspelling.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.530
We have a database
of signs, and we

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:57.570
can swap each signed
tag to an animation

00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:59.610
and hand it back to the user.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.650
And whenever we
don't have a sign,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.640
we do what's actually
done in real life, which

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.780
is to fingerspell the word.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:10.150
If deaf people come by a word
they have never seen before,

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:13.420
like "Google," for instance,
they can fingerspell it.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:18.615
They'll fingerspell G-O-O-G-L-E,
and once they get acquainted

00:13:18.615 --> 00:13:21.160
to it, they come up
with a sign for it.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.690
So that's exactly what our
transition system does.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:30.310
If we find a new word or a
proper noun, like a name,

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:31.830
we can fingerspell it.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:35.340
And if we know that they're
referring to that one

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.340
specific person,
like Barack Obama,

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.160
we can swap that for the sign
instead of finger spelling.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.660
MELANIE WARRICK: I
was going to say,

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:44.219
what's the sign for "Google?"

00:13:44.219 --> 00:13:45.510
THADEU LUZ: I'm not quite sure.

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:45.870
MELANIE WARRICK: No worries.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:46.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.356
THADEU LUZ: Let me
ask Google real quick.

00:13:48.356 --> 00:13:49.980
MELANIE WARRICK:
We're not sure either,

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.390
but I'm sure we will look
it up and find out later.

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:53.190
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.290
OK, so was there
more that you wanted

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:58.140
to explain in terms of
the way you translate?

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:00.111
Because I'm very
curious, especially now

00:14:00.111 --> 00:14:01.860
with where things are
at with neural nets,

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.410
like how you're building
out the translation

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:08.310
from being able to read to have
spoken and written language

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:10.152
translated into
actual animation.

00:14:10.152 --> 00:14:10.860
THADEU LUZ: Yeah.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:13.800
OK, so the animation process
is really straightforward--

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:18.240
just swap the ID by the
animation or fingerspell it.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.930
But the hard part is really
to get the grammar right

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:24.810
for sign language,
because currently we

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:27.120
are using a statistical
machine translation

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:29.637
system that is phrase-based.

00:14:29.637 --> 00:14:31.470
That's what we have
currently in production.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:35.340
But what we really would like
is to have a neural machine

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:39.060
translation system implemented,
especially because we

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:41.730
have the opportunity now
with some tools that Google

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:44.310
has released in the
past couple of months

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:48.540
to have this translation
system deployed to the device,

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.880
to the end user.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:52.980
This last TensorFlow
Summit, they

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.370
released TensorFlow.js,
which is a way

00:14:56.370 --> 00:15:02.130
to direct inference on
the web browser, which

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.260
is amazing in training as well.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:07.920
And Firebase, which is
our primary backend,

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.290
also released a
machine learning kit,

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:12.510
which is also super promising.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.690
There's many things that we
would love to do with it.

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:19.920
And when we nail that neural
machine translation system

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.130
and if it's light enough,
we can allow the user

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.200
to do translations
offline, which

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:29.922
is something that is one of
our top requests for features.

00:15:29.922 --> 00:15:31.380
MELANIE WARRICK:
When we first met,

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.540
we were talking about
how your group went

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:36.130
through the Launchpad
studio program.

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:38.871
You were one of the
initial companies that

00:15:38.871 --> 00:15:41.370
went through this, and this,
of course, is Launchpad Studio.

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:44.550
We had a podcast on that at
the beginning of the year.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.380
What kind of
products and services

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.180
did it give you access
to that you've been

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:49.800
able to use in your company?

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:53.700
THADEU LUZ: We were on the first
class of the Launchpad Studio,

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.480
and our boot camp
was on January 2016.

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:59.460
And it was amazing--

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.450
really, really good.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:03.450
I remember that there
were two things that

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.510
became really clear after that,
and those are two meetings

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:08.590
that we keep going back to.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.100
And one of the
things was when we

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:15.720
did a UX test with someone that
had never used the app before.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:20.100
It sounds really
basic, but we were

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:23.070
able to see where
people were really

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:25.470
struggling to find
information that we

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:27.270
thought was really obvious.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.820
And it's really
enlightening when

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.650
you're able to see
someone that has never

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.970
used your app before trying
to find the things that you

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.190
already know where they are,
and you're not allowed to talk.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.380
So one of the
things that followed

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:43.295
after that was a
redesign of the app

00:16:43.295 --> 00:16:46.330
so that things would be
even clearer to you find

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.480
and that people that
were arriving there

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:52.030
for the first time wouldn't
have to look for them as much.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.140
And the other thing that
was really important for us

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:58.920
in the Launchpad was
that we've been asked

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:00.990
what was the product about?

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:03.390
Who was it trying
to help exactly?

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.990
Is it the person that
uses their oral language

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:08.190
as a first language?

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:10.030
Or is it the deaf user?

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:15.119
And the idea was that, if our
app doesn't do translation

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:17.400
from sign language
to oral language,

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:20.160
how is the deaf user
going to use it?

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:24.450
And are we trying to build a
translator or an education app?

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.069
Because those are two
very different things.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:29.160
If you're trying to
build a translator,

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:33.980
you can just go ahead and
listen to the mic right away.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:36.420
And if you're building
an education app, what

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.960
you want to do is to assess how
much sign language that person

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:43.440
knows and to help him
or her get started

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:47.280
with the first basic signs
that they're going to learn.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:50.970
| we opted for it
being an education app,

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:54.210
because we believe that that's
what's most important for us

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:54.990
to do.

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:56.531
MELANIE WARRICK:
What are the biggest

00:17:56.531 --> 00:17:57.930
challenges with sign language?

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:00.660
THADEU LUZ: Sign language
is really peculiar.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:02.880
Not many people know
this, but sign language

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:07.050
is really, really different
from other kinds of conditions

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:10.440
like blindness or
mobility impairment,

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:13.720
because sign language
creates a culture.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:18.450
And that's the main difference,
because deaf people experience

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:22.110
life in a very different
manner than people that

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:26.250
can hear the life experience
that they have is primarily

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:30.780
visual so everything comes
down to what is seen,

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:33.210
and that's why sign
language is really

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.710
like a natural
language for them.

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:37.320
That's why sign
language feels more

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.970
natural than any other
language for them.

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:43.320
The second reason is
there's one concept, which

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.720
is the concept of
deaf culture, which is

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.700
really important to understand.

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:52.290
When you say that someone is
deaf, with an uppercase D,

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.840
it means that they
are culturally deaf.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.330
It's not only they
are clinically deaf,

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:00.270
they can't hear,
or maybe they can

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:04.020
hear with the aid of cochlear
implants or hearing aids.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:08.820
But what that means is that
they identify with deaf culture.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:11.940
They likely have deaf
friends, and they

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:15.690
enjoy what's called
deaf arts, and they

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:18.010
are aware of deaf history.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:24.140
And there are lots of cultural
manifestations to that.

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:27.600
And one other thing that
deafness is really peculiar

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:31.380
is the fact that sometimes
deaf children struggle

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:33.390
to learn their first
language, and this

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:37.500
is a real, real big
problem because 90% to 95%

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:40.500
of deaf children are born
from hearing parents.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:44.340
And that makes it really
likely that their child

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:48.090
is the very first deaf
person that they ever met.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:51.780
And it is hard to learn sign
language for someone that

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:53.760
came here-- it's really hard.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:56.370
So it is important
for deaf parents

00:19:56.370 --> 00:20:00.140
to have help when they're
trying to learn sign language

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:02.490
and make tools
available for them

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.830
so they can acquire
sign language

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.690
or learn sign language
as much as they

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:08.940
can to have a
better communication

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:10.200
with their children.

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:12.180
There are a couple of
interesting stories

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.400
we have about this.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.260
One of the things that
really makes our day

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:19.140
is when we get
feedback from users,

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:22.770
and they send us emails
and Facebook messages

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:26.880
saying that our app is
helping them have a better

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.050
relationship with
their daughter,

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:33.090
for instance, because they have
a daughter that's 10 years old

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.260
and she's deaf.

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:37.380
She goes to deaf school,
and in school she

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:41.140
can communicate perfectly and
has no problem whatsoever.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:45.330
But when she gets home,
she has no one to talk to.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:47.670
And to hear that
her parents, that

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.150
are using our app to learn sign
language in their spare time

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.820
to be able to better
communicate with their daughter,

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:57.160
is something that
we really value.

00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:59.250
That's why, way
back then when we

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:02.700
were deciding on which
business model to pursue,

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:07.290
we decided that we would not
want to charge for the app

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:08.290
whatsoever.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:15.210
It's not our intention to charge
$5, $6, $10 to a parent that

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.060
wants to learn sign language
so that they can communicate

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.820
with their kid or
a friend that wants

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:24.390
to learn the basic signs
so that they can say hello

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:27.250
to a coworker and
things like that.

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:32.220
So that's why we had the idea of
offering services to companies

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:34.200
so that companies
can pay if they

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.240
want to make one
of their channels

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.092
available also in sign language.

00:21:38.092 --> 00:21:39.300
MARK MANDEL: This is amazing.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:39.500
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.291
MARK MANDEL: And I love
the story about it.

00:21:41.291 --> 00:21:43.770
Just your ethos around the
entire company is phenomenal.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.110
I really would love to also
just get stuck a bit more

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:47.970
into the technical
side of things.

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:51.100
I know you're very
large Firebase users.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.560
Why did you choose Firebase?

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:54.810
What gets you excited
about using that product?

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:57.150
THADEU LUZ: Our
first server version

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.820
was done entirely
by me on Node.js,

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:02.790
and I coded the whole
thing from scratch.

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:08.510
And even though it's not that
hard to make a server use

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:13.270
your system with log, in
log out, reset password,

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:15.920
and create account forms,
and things like that--

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:18.130
it's not that hard--

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:22.710
I find it really important
to be able to have

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:25.710
that information
really secure, to store

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:27.630
the passwords the right way.

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:30.930
And that's not
that trivial, it's

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:33.330
really important
to have security

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:35.850
and to have your app be stable.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:39.140
And that's one of the most
amazing things of all Firebase,

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:41.070
that keeps us coming
back to Firebase,

00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:45.180
is the fact that we can create
user accounts and log in--

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:49.140
there are third-party
authentication systems--

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:51.450
really easily and reliably.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:55.560
And also, one of the things
that we didn't really think that

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:58.620
would benefit as much, which
is a real-time database,

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:02.130
the fact that you can receive
updates from the database

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:03.450
directly.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:05.130
But it's really amazing.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:08.730
It really changes the
perception of the user when,

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:12.000
for instance, they know that
someone is on their website,

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.760
and they can see the
dashboard update instantly

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:16.980
without having to
refresh the page.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:20.410
That's something that's
really, really helpful as well.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:21.870
There are other
things in Firebase

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:24.970
that gets me really
excited about it.

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:27.660
One of them is the fact
that, in 2016, they

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:30.390
released the Google
Cloud Functions, which

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:31.830
is an amazing tool.

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:34.740
Not having to manage
servers is something

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:37.770
that's really valuable to
us, because sometimes we

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:41.910
do have huge spikes in
usage, like when a big news

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:45.660
outlet makes a
blog post about us,

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.780
and we have a huge
spike in translations

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:51.110
and relying on cloud
functions for that.

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.460
And not having to
monitor instances

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:54.700
is really helpful as well.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:57.033
So there are many things in
Firebase that I really like,

00:23:57.033 --> 00:23:59.512
but those three are the
most important, I believe.

00:23:59.512 --> 00:24:00.470
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:03.450
THADEU LUZ: There are also other
Google Cloud Platform services

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:05.690
that we are using that
are really helpful too.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:08.640
OK, one of the hard things
about making a translation

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:12.810
system for sign language is that
data is really hard to come by.

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:16.940
I know that ASL is not as
hard as it is for Libras,

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:20.350
but to find data on Libras
is really, really hard.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:22.020
It would be awesome
if we could do it

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:24.750
just like Google
Translator does,

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:29.430
which is use something like
the European Parliament corpora

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:32.550
or a database of
translator websites,

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:35.650
where you have references
for sometimes two,

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:38.080
sometimes three, four
different languages.

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:41.250
And you use that to feed
your translation system.

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:44.320
But with sign language,
we don't really have that.

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:48.225
We have to create our
own corpus from scratch.

00:24:48.225 --> 00:24:52.480
Our Bitext is done on
a tool that we created,

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:57.120
which actually is heavily
inspired by Google Translate

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.880
community, which is
the web app that you

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:02.280
can contribute to translations.

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:04.360
And we have a system
kind of like that

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:06.210
but for sign language,
where you can also

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:11.190
pick the signs you want or
determine if a certain tag is

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:12.330
fingerspelled.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:17.401
And that's how we produce
the data for our translation

00:25:17.401 --> 00:25:17.900
system.

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:20.233
MELANIE WARRICK: How much
data do you have for training?

00:25:20.233 --> 00:25:23.240
THADEU LUZ: Yeah, our corpus
is around half a million

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:23.920
sentences.

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:27.070
MARK MANDEL: And you're using
Google Cloud for managing that?

00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:28.160
How does that all work?

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.200
THADEU LUZ: OK, so
we use data flow

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:32.630
to clean up and
merge two databases

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:35.660
that we have with
information on our Bitext

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:39.380
with Portuguese and
Brazilian Sign Language.

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:42.980
And we train everything
on Cloud ML, which is also

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:45.960
a huge, huge deal for us.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:47.905
Cloud ML is a godsend.

00:25:47.905 --> 00:25:48.920
MELANIE WARRICK: How so?

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:52.700
THADEU LUZ: As I mentioned, we
have some quite long sentences,

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:56.990
and we have a bunch of
unknown words, like the name.

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:59.930
Many people translate
their names in the app.

00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:03.920
They want to say, my
name is so and so.

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:08.930
And we have many unknown words
of names of entities and proper

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:14.570
nouns, and our system can
get really memory-intensive--

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:16.320
it consumes a lot of memory.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:20.390
And it would be really hard
to train that on our hardware.

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:23.450
I have a MacBook, so it
would be really hard to train

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:26.150
that on hardware on premise.

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:29.480
So we've been using Cloud
ML since the beginning

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:34.340
to iterate and test new
versions of our neural machine

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:37.340
translation system, and it's
working really, really well.

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:38.362
It's amazing.

00:26:38.362 --> 00:26:40.070
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
this is wonderful.

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:42.140
So one question I
have for you is,

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:45.410
I know we were saying how
it's focused on Portuguese

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:47.190
and Brazilian Sign Language.

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:48.740
What are your future
plans in terms

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:50.782
of sign language translation?

00:26:50.782 --> 00:26:52.490
THADEU LUZ: We have
many features that we

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:54.470
would like to do on our app.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:58.490
Some of them on the short term,
like American Sign Language,

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:01.760
but some of them are going to
take a bit longer to develop

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:03.290
the technology for them.

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:06.440
American Sign Language
we would like to be

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:07.910
done by the end of the year.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:11.140
But we're closing a round
of funding right now,

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:13.470
and it's taken a little bit
longer than we expected.

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:17.210
We have to run the numbers again
to have a final date for when

00:27:17.210 --> 00:27:18.490
the ASL is going to be ready.

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:20.240
MELANIE WARRICK: So
American Sign Language

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:21.906
is one that you're
targeting, hopefully,

00:27:21.906 --> 00:27:24.080
by the end of the
year, depending on how

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:25.700
things are going with funding.

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:28.760
And other languages other
features that you're

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:30.009
interested in pursuing?

00:27:30.009 --> 00:27:31.550
THADEU LUZ: One of
the other features

00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:33.680
that we really would
love to implement

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:35.780
is doing it the
other way around.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:39.230
Currently we only do oral
language to sign language,

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:43.040
but we would love to do sign
language to oral language,

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:46.820
to be able to use the device
camera to convert images

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:48.050
to oral language.

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:50.740
That would be something
very big for us.

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:53.030
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed,
especially with a way machine

00:27:53.030 --> 00:27:56.000
learning is evolving from
object identification

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:59.330
and other classification and
predictive tooling around image

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:00.440
analysis.

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:01.370
This is wonderful.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:04.940
Thadeu, if people out there
are interested in contributing

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:08.630
to your company and contributing
to the product, what are ways

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:10.190
that they can get involved?

00:28:10.190 --> 00:28:11.900
THADEU LUZ: OK, I
thought of three things

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:13.940
that might be interesting.

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:19.070
One is that you know ASL or you
represent an institution that

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:20.930
deals with ASL.

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:25.310
You can get in touch with us to
see how we can either come up

00:28:25.310 --> 00:28:28.940
with a partnership to help
improve our ASL translation

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:29.960
system.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:32.690
So you can get in touch with
me on Twitter @thadeuluz.

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.670
And also, if you're
a data scientist

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:44.920
and you are aware of language
labeled or unlabelled

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.630
data sets for ASL
that are open, you

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.700
can send a link
to me, because I'm

00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:52.040
pretty sure there are quite
a few data sets that I'm not

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:53.693
aware of yet on ASL.

00:28:53.693 --> 00:28:56.030
And I'm looking for
them, collecting

00:28:56.030 --> 00:28:59.270
them to know exactly
which ones we

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:03.200
can use to speed up the
process of coming up with data.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:06.530
And if you're just curious about
sign language and would love

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:11.090
to hear from us when we release
the ASL version of our app,

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:16.700
you can go to handtalk.me/asl
and sign up to our newsletter

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:17.420
there.

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:19.070
And you're going
to be the first one

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.250
to know when we have the app
ready for English and American

00:29:22.250 --> 00:29:22.834
Sign Language.

00:29:22.834 --> 00:29:24.375
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, this is great.

